<id/02/0290/#concept> a <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E55_Type>, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Isinglass"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch <customs-1866/fish_part_collagen/#concept> ;
    skos:broader <id/grp/grp_fertilizers_animal_materials/#concept> ;
    skos:closeMatch <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300014822> ;
    skos:definition "or Fish-glue, called in Latin _ichthyocolla_, is a whitish, dry, tough, semi-transparent substance, twisted into different shapes, often in the form of a lyre, and consisting of membranes rolled together. Good isinglass is unchangeable in the air, has a leathery aspect, and a mawkish taste nearly insipid; when steeped in cold water it swells, softens, and separates in membranous laminæ. At the boiling heat it dissolves in water, and the solution, on cooling, forms a white jelly, which is semi-transparent, soluble in weak acids, but is precipitated from them by alkalies. It is gelatine nearly pure; and if not brittle, like other glue, this depends on its fibrous and elastic texture. [Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562)]"@en ;
